1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:07,155
A woman started dancing in the street... and she didn't stop. Within weeks, hundreds of people joined her.

2
00:00:11,617 --> 00:00:18,426
Summer of 1518. Strasbourg, a city in today's France, on a hot July day.

3
00:00:18,658 --> 00:00:24,290
On a crowded street, a woman named Frau Troffea suddenly starts to dance.

4
00:00:24,658 --> 00:00:28,001
There is no music, no wedding, no festival.

5
00:00:29,092 --> 00:00:34,854
She isn't smiling. Her face shows pain. But she won't stop.

6
00:00:34,825 --> 00:00:39,069
And this will be the start of one of history's strangest epidemics.

7
00:00:40,533 --> 00:00:44,990
Hours pass. Frau Troffea is still dancing.

8
00:00:45,658 --> 00:00:50,349
Her feet are bleeding, her body is shaking, but she doesn't stop.

9
00:00:50,492 --> 00:00:53,728
At night, she collapses and falls asleep.

10
00:00:55,075 --> 00:00:59,482
And when she wakes up in the morning, she gets up and dances again.

11
00:01:00,283 --> 00:01:02,463
This goes on for days.

12
00:01:04,867 --> 00:01:11,082
According to sources, within a few days neighbors, passers-by and curious people come to watch her.

13
00:01:12,533 --> 00:01:16,012
Then, something no one expected happens.

14
00:01:16,867 --> 00:01:20,546
One of the onlookers suddenly starts dancing with her.

15
00:01:22,200 --> 00:01:24,497
Then another. And then another.

16
00:01:26,783 --> 00:01:32,352
Within a week, more than 30 people are dancing nonstop in the streets.

17
00:01:32,658 --> 00:01:37,300
Men, women, young and old. Rich and poor alike.

18
00:01:39,408 --> 00:01:46,213
According to a city chronicle, within a month the number of dancers reaches 400.

19
00:01:46,242 --> 00:01:48,693
The city's leaders don't know what to do.

20
00:01:50,033 --> 00:01:54,413
They ask the doctors. The doctors' answer is surprising:

21
00:01:54,617 --> 00:02:00,173
This is not a curse, but a disease. The cause: overheated blood.

22
00:02:00,575 --> 00:02:03,283
And the cure? Even more dancing!

23
00:02:05,158 --> 00:02:09,258
The city council turns the guild halls into dance halls.

24
00:02:09,742 --> 00:02:14,472
Stages are built in the market squares, and musicians are hired for the dancers.

25
00:02:15,533 --> 00:02:20,297
The idea was simple: let them dance until the fever breaks, and they'll be cured.

26
00:02:21,575 --> 00:02:23,826
But the plan backfired.

27
00:02:26,158 --> 00:02:30,397
Seeing the music and the crowds, even more people joined the dance.

28
00:02:31,033 --> 00:02:35,096
As the summer went on, things turned terrible.

29
00:02:35,408 --> 00:02:39,547
The dancers were exhausted, and some collapsed to the ground.

30
00:02:40,075 --> 00:02:44,173
Some sources say that for a while, people were dying every day.

31
00:02:44,908 --> 00:02:50,585
But historians remind us that most of these accounts of deaths were written after the event.

32
00:02:51,533 --> 00:02:55,939
What is certain is that the city was gripped by deep fear.

33
00:02:56,533 --> 00:03:00,429
Among the people, a very different explanation was spreading.

34
00:03:00,867 --> 00:03:04,180
This was not a disease. It was a curse.

35
00:03:05,450 --> 00:03:07,363
The curse of Saint Vitus.

36
00:03:10,033 --> 00:03:16,210
According to the beliefs of the time, anyone who angered Saint Vitus was doomed to dance without end.

37
00:03:16,908 --> 00:03:19,195
This time, the city council changed its mind.

38
00:03:21,492 --> 00:03:27,217
The stages were torn down. Music and dancing were almost completely banned.

39
00:03:27,117 --> 00:03:31,659
The dancers were put on carts and taken to the shrine of Saint Vitus in the mountains.

40
00:03:33,700 --> 00:03:39,161
There, they were given small crosses and red shoes were put on their feet.

41
00:03:40,117 --> 00:03:44,174
And according to sources, the epidemic slowly came to an end.

42
00:03:44,950 --> 00:03:47,330
So what really happened?

43
00:03:49,283 --> 00:03:52,769
Some scientists blamed a poison from moldy rye.

44
00:03:54,408 --> 00:04:00,025
But this poison cuts off blood flow. Dancing for days with it would be nearly impossible.

45
00:04:00,992 --> 00:04:05,005
Historian John Waller offered another explanation.

46
00:04:07,575 --> 00:04:11,770
The years before 1518 had been terrible for Strasbourg.

47
00:04:13,283 --> 00:04:18,753
Bad harvests, famine, epidemics, and endless fear.

48
00:04:20,283 --> 00:04:27,904
According to Waller, when such huge stress met a strong belief in the curse, people fell into a kind of trance.

49
00:04:28,867 --> 00:04:32,985
One person's fear passed on to the next. Just like a yawn.

50
00:04:33,867 --> 00:04:36,792
Today, we call this mass psychogenic illness.

51
00:04:40,450 --> 00:04:42,811
And it wasn't the only case.

52
00:04:47,033 --> 00:04:52,129
Over the centuries, dozens of similar dancing epidemics were recorded along the Rhine in Europe.

53
00:04:54,533 --> 00:05:03,460
In 1962, a laughing epidemic that began at a school in Tanganyika, in Africa, spread to hundreds of people.

54
00:05:03,492 --> 00:05:07,491
The dancing plague shows how powerful the human mind can be.

55
00:05:09,117 --> 00:05:15,031
When fear, belief and stress come together, they can even take control of our bodies.

56
00:05:15,950 --> 00:05:22,435
And even today, scientists still debate what exactly happened in Strasbourg in the summer of 1518.

57
00:05:23,675 --> 00:05:31,386
What do you think happened in Strasbourg: a disease, a curse, or the power of fear? Let us know in the comments.
